WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:07.310
In the fifth episode of the original series
of Star Trek, the show tries to pass off what

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:12.250
is clearly someone’s dog with a horn taped
to its head as an ‘alien lifeform.’

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:17.420
It’s pretty hilarious to revisit — not
just to see the actors make a heroic attempt

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:22.760
to stay in-character while carrying this thing
around — but also because this unicorn dog

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:27.439
doesn’t seem distinct enough from life on
earth to make a believable alien.

00:00:27.439 --> 00:00:32.910
But this raises an interesting question: if
there is life elsewhere in the universe, just

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.420
how ‘alien’ will aliens look?

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:41.210
It’s a question many scientists and sci-fi
authors have speculated on, and is a fascinating

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:43.079
rabbit hole to go down.

00:00:43.079 --> 00:00:48.840
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
explore relevant phenomena in evolution and

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:54.320
physics to determine if extraterrestrials
are more likely to look like Earth animals,

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.280
or something far stranger...

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:02.790
For our first example, let’s start by imagining
an Earth-like planet.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:07.650
It’s got a comfortable amount of liquid
water, a protective atmosphere, and plenty

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:11.610
of carbon to form the building-blocks of organic
compounds.

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:16.799
If life emerges on such a world, what kinds
of ecosystems will form?

00:01:16.799 --> 00:01:22.310
One of the most important phenomena to consider
when imagining extraterrestrial life is something

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:28.700
called convergent evolution — or the independent
evolution of similar features across different

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:32.960
species despite being part of different evolutionary
branches.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:38.750
It’s why echidnas and hedgehogs look almost
identical, despite one being a marsupial and

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:40.650
the other a placental mammal.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:46.270
Or why hummingbirds and hummingbird hawk-moths
are often mistaken for each other, despite

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:50.540
their last common ancestor predating the first
land animal.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:56.360
Or why crustaceans simply refuse to stop evolving
into crabs — a trend we’ll discuss more

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:58.149
in just a bit.

00:01:58.149 --> 00:02:03.450
With all these examples, it feels like nature
is copying its own homework, and not changing

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.670
things enough to get away with it.

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:10.580
But evolution isn’t a conscious force, though
we often think of it that way: it’s the

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:16.120
product of random genetic mutations allowing
certain members of a species to better survive

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:19.900
and pass on their genes, resulting in change
over time.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:25.069
And a trait that benefits one species might
also benefit another in a different time or

00:02:25.069 --> 00:02:28.590
place if they face similar obstacles.

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:35.099
One of the best examples of convergent evolution
is how sharks — a type of fish, dolphins

00:02:35.099 --> 00:02:40.720
— a type of marine mammal, and ichthyosaurs
— a type of extinct marine reptile, all

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.819
evolved the same general body shape despite
coming from vastly different lineages.

00:02:45.819 --> 00:02:51.709
They all have streamlined bodies, tail flukes,
and dorsal fins that are in roughly the same

00:02:51.709 --> 00:02:52.709
place.

00:02:52.709 --> 00:02:57.550
This is particularly remarkable for dolphins
and ichthyosaurs, as neither of them started

00:02:57.550 --> 00:02:59.990
off with any fins at all.

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:04.940
As is often the case, the main reason for
this seemingly miraculous convergence has

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:06.690
to do with physics.

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:11.599
Simulations have shown these body shapes are
the optimal way of moving through the dense

00:03:11.599 --> 00:03:13.269
medium of water.

00:03:13.269 --> 00:03:18.909
So, natural selection encouraged parallel
attributes in each lineage, with the three

00:03:18.909 --> 00:03:23.670
animals essentially finding similar solutions
to the same problem.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:29.239
So, on a habitable alien planet with liquid
oceans, perhaps we’d find lifeforms with

00:03:29.239 --> 00:03:32.150
the same general body plan swimming beneath
the waves…

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:40.349
A more extreme example of convergent evolution
is how crustaceans just won’t stop evolving

00:03:40.349 --> 00:03:41.349
into crabs.

00:03:41.349 --> 00:03:43.319
I told you we’d get to it.

00:03:43.319 --> 00:03:48.111
The number of times different lineages have
independently converged on a crab-like body

00:03:48.111 --> 00:03:54.510
plan is so high that there’s even a word
for it: carcinization, meaning to become crab-like.

00:03:54.510 --> 00:04:00.580
Today, there is a staggering array of these
crab mimics across the globe, from the tiny

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:05.879
strawberry hermit crab that feeds on seaweed,
to the coconut crab that looks like it feeds

00:04:05.879 --> 00:04:06.920
on your nightmares.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:11.909
I’ve made jokes about carcinization before
on my channel, because there is something

00:04:11.909 --> 00:04:14.250
oddly hilarious about the phenomenon.

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:17.769
Are crabs just the pinnacle of evolution?

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:22.900
What is it about the crab body-shape that
makes so many unrelated crustaceans seemingly

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:25.380
jealous enough to copy their style?

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:28.500
Well, once again physics is our culprit.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:33.190
The long tails of crustaceans like shrimp
or lobsters can restrict the species’ mobility.

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:39.750
By contrast, the compact crab-shape is efficient
and versatile — with few exposed parts that

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:42.320
predators can get to without getting pinched.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:48.000
So, there’s nothing mystical about why the
crab body plan is so trendy — though it’s

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.830
fun to think so -- it’s simply a good answer
to the question of survival, and likely would

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.610
be on other Earth-like planets as well...

00:04:55.610 --> 00:05:03.750
But these are all examples of convergence
in species that already have a lot in common

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:07.280
— like eyes, legs, and a skeletal structure.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:12.860
If microbial life were to emerge on an Earth-like
planet, would it really end up evolving these

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:13.860
structures?

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:19.150
In his book ‘A Zoologist’s Guide to the
Galaxy,’ Cambridge zoologist Arik Kershenbaum

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:25.280
reflects on how certain features like eyes
and limbs might be more common across extraterrestrial

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:26.879
life than we’d think.

00:05:26.879 --> 00:05:32.509
The eye might seem like a unique feature aliens
wouldn’t necessarily converge upon.

00:05:32.509 --> 00:05:38.380
But on our planet, eyes are thought to have
evolved multiple times across different lineages.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:44.050
Eyes likely emerge from rudimentary photoreceptive
organs, which some lifeforms on Earth still

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:49.790
possess, and then through natural selection
become more complex over millions of years.

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:55.010
Yet across these variations, eyes always serve
the same essential navigational function.

00:05:55.010 --> 00:06:00.740
And on another planet, a creature’s chance
of survival will also likely be higher if

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:02.740
they can see where they’re going.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:08.960
So, if the first aliens we encounter end up
having eyes, it won’t be that surprising.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:11.080
The same can be said for legs.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:16.210
On earth, arthropods and vertebrates evolved
legs independently from one another after

00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:17.930
emerging from the water.

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:23.350
While the shape and number of legs might fluctuate,
the mechanical advantages legs offer are likely

00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:27.530
to be universal across Earth-like planets.

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:33.290
Even more fundamentally, practically all animal
life on Earth exhibits Cephalization, or the

00:06:33.290 --> 00:06:38.750
concentration of the mouth, sense organs,
and nerve ganglia on the front end of the

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:41.870
organism, producing a head region.

00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:46.830
Cephalization is likely the simple result
of the advantage that having the part of the

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:50.699
animal that goes forward be the one fitted
out with sense organs.

00:06:50.699 --> 00:06:55.790
And on most planets, it’ll likely always
be more important to know where you are going

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:58.729
than where you have been.

00:06:58.729 --> 00:07:01.360
Even plants convergently evolve.

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:06.630
Trees — that is to say, large photosynthetic
lifeforms protected by a bark-like substance

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:10.900
— have emerged many times from different
plant lineages.

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:16.151
The evolution of fruits like apples happened
independently from fruits like tomatoes, with

00:07:16.151 --> 00:07:20.789
both of these shiny red fruits emerging to
get the attention of animals that disperse

00:07:20.789 --> 00:07:23.520
their seeds after chowing down.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:29.310
Likewise, seed dispersal by ants has evolved
more than one-hundred times, and is present

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:34.830
across more than eleven thousand plant species
— making it one of the most dramatic examples

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:38.780
of convergent evolution in all biology.

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:43.310
Yet convergent evolution doesn’t always
produce organisms that look the same.

00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:49.140
Take, for example, the woodpecker — a kind
of bird, and the aye-aye — a type of lemur.

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:54.930
Both of these animals occupy the same basic
role as predators who feed on insects that

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:57.259
hide within trees.

00:07:57.259 --> 00:08:01.919
Woodpeckers get to these insects using their
beaks, and aye-ayes get to these insects using

00:08:01.919 --> 00:08:05.360
their elongated, somewhat unsettling finger.

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:09.470
In terms of their lifestyle, these animals
are quite similar, but you’d still never

00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:11.509
mistake one for the other.

00:08:11.509 --> 00:08:17.040
So, even if an alien occupied a similar niche
to an earth animal, there’s not always a

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:19.210
guarantee they’d look alike.

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:25.630
So, on our Earth-like alien planet, we might
find mobile animal-like organisms with eyes

00:08:25.630 --> 00:08:31.699
and limbs moving around stationary plant-like
lifeform, occupying niches that are familiar

00:08:31.699 --> 00:08:32.699
to us.

00:08:32.699 --> 00:08:37.280
But they might still appear pretty distinct
from life on Earth, and certainly aren’t

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:40.060
likely to look like the Star Trek dog.

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:43.750
Still a majestic creature, though.

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:49.450
But what about the possibility of aliens with
intelligence comparable to ours?

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:55.240
Would they evolve a convergent form on an
Earth-like planet, or would they look entirely

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:56.240
different?

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:00.511
The question of what ‘intelligence’ even
means could probably be its own video, but

00:09:00.511 --> 00:09:02.990
for now, let’s keep things simple.

00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:08.889
Returning to Star Trek, Spock is certainly
a… suspiciously humanoid extraterrestrial

00:09:08.889 --> 00:09:15.320
— with an appearance identical to humans
with the exception of elf ears and funky eyebrows.

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:19.600
This was more the result of budget constraints
than anything else, but it’s a trend you

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:21.750
see across a lot of science fiction.

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:27.450
And while it’s easy to assume that an intelligent
alien would look like we do, that’s not

00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:29.970
necessarily the case.

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:34.910
Human hands might seem like a tool perfectly
designed for crafting technology, but our

00:09:34.910 --> 00:09:39.110
hands didn’t originally evolve for manipulating
objects.

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:43.860
The digits we use to work our smart phones
began as a means to better grip on to the

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:46.480
trees we used to swing through.

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:51.940
Aliens would probably need complex manipulatory
appendages to create technology, sure, but

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:54.730
that could take any number of divergent forms.

00:09:54.730 --> 00:10:00.610
Likewise, our bipedal stature might seem ideal,
but ask any orthopedic doctor what they think

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:06.420
of our spines, and you’ll realize the human
body plan has some definite drawbacks extraterrestrials

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:08.290
might want to sidestep.

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:13.580
Of course, some things seem likely to be universal
across advanced life in the galaxy.

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:16.579
They’d probably need a large brain, right?

00:10:16.579 --> 00:10:21.660
Well, that might not even be the case, as
crows have relatively tiny brains but are

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:25.530
able to solve logical puzzles and use simple
tools.

00:10:25.530 --> 00:10:30.640
Their dense brain structure means they actually
have a similar number of total neurons to

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:35.990
that of primates, and indicates that alien
intellects really might not need to resemble

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:41.900
the human form after all--unless they also
happened to evolve from a primate-like species

00:10:41.900 --> 00:10:44.100
that began walking upright.

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:49.140
With all the planets in the universe, intelligent
humanoid aliens aren’t impossible, just

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:51.100
not all that likely.

00:10:51.100 --> 00:10:56.490
But it’s time we addressed the fact that
not all planets are the same.

00:10:56.490 --> 00:11:01.810
An earth-like level of gravity, for example,
isn’t a constant throughout the universe.

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:08.600
Our planet’s gravitational pull is an invisible
yet crucial influence across evolution on

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:14.670
Earth, affecting the parameters of animals’
body plans, maximum size, and behavior.

00:11:14.670 --> 00:11:21.660
So, on a planet where gravity is, say, significantly
weaker, evolution might take a very different

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:22.730
path.

00:11:22.730 --> 00:11:28.360
One of the best depictions of low-gravity
alien life comes from Sam Vilasboas’s speculative

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:32.800
work of Planet Anu, which I’ve covered before
on the channel.

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:38.040
This evolutionary thought experiment depicts
extraterrestrials that take advantage of their

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:43.149
planet’s weak gravitational pull in a variety
of ways — with many having tall, spindly

00:11:43.149 --> 00:11:46.870
bodies that wouldn’t be able to carry their
weight on our planet.

00:11:46.870 --> 00:11:52.501
Vilasboas does imagine some convergence with
Earth life would take place on such a world

00:11:52.501 --> 00:11:57.980
— but there are also a variety of lifeforms
our planet could never support.

00:11:57.980 --> 00:12:03.279
On the other side of the spectrum, we have
the possibility of alien life evolving on

00:12:03.279 --> 00:12:07.510
a world with a stronger gravitational pull
than our own.

00:12:07.510 --> 00:12:12.300
Christian Cline’s speculative field guide
to alien life, the Teeming Universe, which

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:17.579
I’ve also covered before on the channel,
gives us a look at a high gravity world occupied

00:12:17.579 --> 00:12:20.470
by heavy, slow-moving lifeforms.

00:12:20.470 --> 00:12:26.100
On the fictional exoplanet Borold, Cline imagines
extraterrestrials that creep slowly across

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:31.320
the ground like giant terrestrial starfish,
their bodies built to withstand over three

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:35.410
times the gravitational forces we experience
here on Earth.

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:41.180
And dissimilar gravity isn’t the only variable
that might make life on exoplanets different

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:42.560
from what we’re familiar with.

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:43.560
Atmosphere is another thing to consider.

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:46.450
Remember how I mentioned alien life might
have eyes?

00:12:46.450 --> 00:12:52.040
Well, on a planet with an opaque atmosphere
where energy comes not from a sun but from,

00:12:52.040 --> 00:12:56.450
say, geothermal vents, you might see a world
of sightless creatures.

00:12:56.450 --> 00:13:01.970
On Earth, many deep-sea creatures have either
undeveloped eyes or no eyes at all.

00:13:01.970 --> 00:13:06.649
So, it makes sense that on an alien world
with very little light, there wouldn’t be

00:13:06.649 --> 00:13:11.320
much point in having light-receptive organs
— and organisms might navigate through their

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:14.709
sense of smell or through advanced echolocation.

00:13:14.709 --> 00:13:21.660
And even if a planet’s surface is inhospitable,
maybe some organisms could thrive in a planet’s

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:22.660
atmosphere.

00:13:22.660 --> 00:13:27.279
Returning to the Teeming Universe, there’s
a section of the book that outlines the possibility

00:13:27.279 --> 00:13:32.980
of life emerging in the stratosphere of a
planet somewhere between a terrestrial world

00:13:32.980 --> 00:13:35.100
and a gas giant like Jupiter.

00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:40.589
The chapter depicts extraterrestrials that
float on sacs of internal hydrogen, steering

00:13:40.589 --> 00:13:43.850
themselves through the air on specialized
fins.

00:13:43.850 --> 00:13:49.360
The possibility of life within the atmosphere
of a gas giant has been entertained by numerous

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:53.160
scientists over the years, including Carl
Sagan.

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:58.420
When considering evolution on planets different
from our own, extraterrestrial life becomes

00:13:58.420 --> 00:13:59.880
less and less familiar...

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:04.589
And things could get weirder.

00:14:04.589 --> 00:14:10.240
Different planetary parameters aren’t the
only factor when considering how ‘alien’

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:11.769
extraterrestrial life might be.

00:14:11.769 --> 00:14:17.170
So far, we’ve been assuming alien organisms
would have similar fundamental chemistry to

00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:20.329
our own — but that might not be the case.

00:14:20.329 --> 00:14:25.389
Crystalline life based on the silicon atom
— instead of carbon like life on earth — has

00:14:25.389 --> 00:14:30.350
been a topic of discussion among scientists
since before the 20th century.

00:14:30.350 --> 00:14:35.570
Since silicon-based bonds are most stable
at high temperatures, such life might theoretically

00:14:35.570 --> 00:14:39.050
thrive on planets with high geothermal activity.

00:14:39.050 --> 00:14:44.899
Even if we visited such planets, we might
not recognize them as full of life at a glance,

00:14:44.899 --> 00:14:48.589
since such ecosystems might seem barren to
our eyes.

00:14:48.589 --> 00:14:54.610
Now, more recent studies have shown there
are some potential chemical pitfalls to silicon-based

00:14:54.610 --> 00:14:55.610
life.

00:14:55.610 --> 00:14:59.910
But it’s difficult to disprove such a theoretical
concept with any certainty.

00:14:59.910 --> 00:15:04.980
Who knows, maybe somewhere out there in the
universe a silicon-based scientist is working

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:09.070
on an article about how carbon-based life
just isn’t possible.

00:15:09.070 --> 00:15:14.600
In fact, some Earth animals have been observed
using silicon in their structures.

00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:19.940
Diatoms are tiny phytoplankton that encase
themselves in a cell wall made of silicate.

00:15:19.940 --> 00:15:25.340
Now, these organisms aren’t silicon based,
but they do show the material’s potential

00:15:25.340 --> 00:15:27.980
to be used in biological life.

00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:30.550
And why stop at silicon?

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:36.279
Scientists at Cornell University have modeled
a theoretical oxygen-free cell based on methane

00:15:36.279 --> 00:15:40.160
that could be the basis for some really unusual
lifeforms.

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:46.100
Protected by a cell membrane called an ‘azotosome’
able to withstand extreme temperatures, these

00:15:46.100 --> 00:15:51.940
hypothetical cells could potentially survive
on exoplanets with weak atmospheres.

00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:56.920
And going to the most extreme scenario, it’s
possible there are lifeforms that our limited

00:15:56.920 --> 00:15:59.850
view of the universe hasn’t prepared us
for.

00:15:59.850 --> 00:16:05.329
When it comes to extraterrestrials, we don’t
know what we don’t know, and maybe our definition

00:16:05.329 --> 00:16:11.600
of life will prove too limited in the infinite
variations of the cosmos.

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:14.380
So, where does all this leave us?

00:16:14.380 --> 00:16:19.779
Well, the truth is, we can’t definitely
say what aliens do or do not look like without

00:16:19.779 --> 00:16:21.550
ever having met any.

00:16:21.550 --> 00:16:27.259
We can make inferences based on evolution
on Earth, but with a sample size of one, it’s

00:16:27.259 --> 00:16:32.160
difficult to say how much we really know about
how life tends to emerge.

00:16:32.160 --> 00:16:37.290
With so many planets, it’s not a leap to
imagine that some alien ecosystems would be

00:16:37.290 --> 00:16:42.279
somewhat similar to Earth life, while others
would be completely different.

00:16:42.279 --> 00:16:46.759
So, if you’re a worldbuilder or writer who’s
worried their fictional creatures are too

00:16:46.759 --> 00:16:52.180
similar to Earth life, remember that nature
itself isn’t always completely original.

00:16:52.180 --> 00:16:56.759
And if you’re worried your fictional life
is too out there, remember that we are only

00:16:56.759 --> 00:16:59.899
just beginning to discover what’s possible.

00:16:59.899 --> 00:17:05.340
Maybe one day we’ll find an ecosystem much
like our own, or an ecosystem that stretches

00:17:05.340 --> 00:17:07.059
the definition of life.

00:17:07.059 --> 00:17:11.260
